WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.350
Good afternoon.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.180
So many of you probably
have seen or tried

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.970
to help a workplace adopt R.
So this is the typical case

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.190
I see.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.940
Your company hired an R trainer

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.310
who went over a lot of
fancy things possible

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.090
in R with the team.
After the training, everyone

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.130
finds R very cool.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.760
The tidyverse amazing.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.290
R Markdown awesome.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.140
Shiny fantastic.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.119
But then after a few
days, most people

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.890
just went back to the go-to tools.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.620
I'd rather not call it out,
but something that's not R.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.050
Several years ago, my
company moved to R

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.900
and we face the same
situation. To help change

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.200
that, I initiated s work
automation project with R,

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.550
and now we have 20 plus
team members who consider

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.770
R the go-to tools, 60 plus
stakeholders who use

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.720
R product on a daily basis.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.160
And this has saved over
12,000 hours and counting.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.230
In the next 15
minutes, I'm going

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.930
to talk about key parts of
the project, lessons learned

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.730
and the structure
you could follow

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.600
if you'd like to implement it.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.300
Now let's revisit the situation.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.380
We want to move things to R but
why on their many movements?

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:18.790
Well, we all have learning
curve as beginners.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.550
Some of us went
through it quicker.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.360
More of us don't.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.960
The other scenario I can think
of is that after the training,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.260
we're not asked to
handle the right tasks.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.940
Some of those tasks
might not even

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.590
be covered in the training.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.050
They may look similar though.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.510
Even if you were able
to avoid the first two,

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.330
you may still end up
in the last situation

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.340
where you have a
grand plan of what

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.920
to do with your
project using R,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.290
but had to go back
to the old ways

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.130
because your deadline
is the same. Now,

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.410
unfortunately, before
realizing any of these,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.530
we assume everything will
work after the training.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.720
When it didn't, we
just thought we need

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.090
to work harder on the next one.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.780
In fact, in one of those
follow up training I provided.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.740
I even remade the Lion King song
into Can You Use the R Tonight,

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.020
summarizing the functions
we talked about, but it still

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.440
did not help.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.210
Turns out what do we need is
to create a soft landing that

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:21.140
will start small and help
the team to build confidence.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.350
Identify the task
for them and plan

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.690
the effort for the transition.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.200
My soft landing idea then was
to build a work automation

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.550
project using R.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.440
And next, I'm going
to talk about the six

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.860
key parts of the project.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.310
And in the end, you
will see why that

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.190
was perfect for this situation.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.310
So to build an
automation project first,

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.310
let's look into what's possible.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.360
At that time, I was a
member of a data team,

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.390
and for most data
teams on this planet,

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.970
you cannot escape from reports.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.220
So let's take a look
at a typical report.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.270
If we can describe a step by
step, like traveling on a map.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.270
You first

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:06.530
open your app, maybe SQL
database, data lake, all that.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.850
Then you update your
parameter and run.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.230
Then you wait.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.270
Export the data,
clean the results,

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.500
analyze it, update the format,
draft an email and send.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.040
But then if you look again, it
almost looks like all of these steps

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.760
can be divided into three
portions where we just

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.448
use different groups
of applications.

00:03:28.448 --> 00:03:29.490
For some of those reports,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.880
I would start with
Oracle, move on to Excel

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.430
and then end it with
Outlook. For other reports,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.360
I would just use
other applications,

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.850
but regardless of the difference
of those applications,

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.580
we all seem to start
from getting the data,

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.790
then doing the data wrangling,
analysis, visualization,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.230
and end it with communication.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.950
So if you have a report
that have any parts that

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.600
fits any of those
three portions,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.270
we can replace that
portion with R code

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.320
with the help of those R packages.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.340
And the good thing is there are
so many of these R packages.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.280
Now, this makes
sense for the reports

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.870
that would take some efforts.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.250
But how about

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.820
the process where the pain point
was running the test itself

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.732
and sharing the result
on a daily basis?

00:04:16.732 --> 00:04:18.149
How about the
stakeholder request

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:22.460
where we just require you to run
a SQL query and share the data?

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.500
Well, they still
fit in the scope

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.180
of this automation project.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:30.560
We just need a little bit more
help. When defining automation,

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.880
there are three types.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.980
So the first type
or the one perfect

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:39.200
for the reports that will still
need some human involvement.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.880
And that would be perfect
to be done with R code.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.470
The second type
are the ones that

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.840
are good for the process
that does not necessarily

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.820
need human input, but will need
to happen at a certain time

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.350
and would be best with R
plus RStudio Connect.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.740
Lastly, also my favorite, a
combination of the previous two.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.930
In this case,

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:03.700
the human input will
come from stakeholders

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.770
and they can kick off
whatever processes

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.440
that will get them the answer

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.650
with the help of Shiny.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.820
Now if we look at these

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.280
definitions, none
of those are new,

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.190
but the current R
ecosystem was able to offer

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.410
a brand new interpretation.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.870
So after knowing what can
be done in the project

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.720
and what the current R
ecosystem can help you with,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.490
time to sell it to
decision makers.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.680
And whenever you're
selling something,

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.810
you want to talk
about its benefits.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.690
So what are the benefits here?

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.340
Reproducibility- the first
word to pop in my head.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.830
Then, we may get
less human error

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.600
if we code everything correctly.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.550
And if we were able to
achieve the first two,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.100
we to be able to save some time.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.200
So I got a few decision
makers in the room, presented

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.110
them my plan of automating
all the tasks identified

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.230
in step one, along
with the benefits here.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.330
If I could replicate
the third interest

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.790
level using the gauge here.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.920
So when I first talk
about reproducibility,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.370
I gave them an example.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.130
If I'm out of
office, someone else

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.010
can run all the tasks
that I'm handling.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:17.010
Their interest level
wasn't very high, maybe

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.100
because it sounded very
similar to a travel insurance

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.380
where it is not
urgent at the moment.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.270
You would only miss it
after things happen.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:29.620
Then I moved on with less human error
but not additional interest

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.040
because maybe they are
thinking it is all theoretical

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.450
and code is written by human too.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.680
Lastly, hours saved. More interest.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.300
And then they even asked
me if I have any numbers,

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.210
but overall I did
not get the go ahead.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.420
This is when I realize
I was so ready and so

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.860
prepared to sell this whole
thing to an R user, who

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.170
is more concerned about
the day to day workflow,

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:57.830
but not decision makers who
have no programming experience

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:01.160
and who is more concerned
about the return on investment

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.140
as well as business urgencies.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.080
So I wait a few weeks
just after everybody

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.590
forgot about what I said,
and updated my strategy

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.750
and this is what I presented.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.030
So I started with hours saved.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.180
In fact, I told them, if
we go through with this,

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.540
we might be able to save
1,000 hours per year.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.070
They're interested.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.660
Then I moved on to less

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.400
human error

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.640
if we code everything correctly.
They're still intrigued.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.810
Lastly, I sold
reproducibility like a free add

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:34.400
on travel insurance.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.320
Who wouldn't love that?

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:38.090
Overall, we got the go ahead.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.040
But then if you look
back, it is still

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.990
the same number
of benefits, just

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:43.700
in a slightly different order.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.410
I guess sometimes it
makes sense for us

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.230
to start with the one
that does not require

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.000
too much context to understand.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.390
And now you see why I name
the topic saving 1,000 hours

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.977
with RStudio, not bringing
reproducibility with RStudio.

00:07:56.977 --> 00:07:59.060
If that was the talk theme, I
don't know if half of you

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.970
would show up or even
I would show up.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:05.690
So after I get the
decision makers buy in, time

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:09.530
to get the things we need
to do the actual automation.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.170
High level are two
things a document

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.200
of the current process

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.980
so that we know what needs to
be done, and information for us

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.670
to compare all the tasks

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.210
so that we know which
one to start with.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.340
To get the
documentation, we will

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.800
need to understand
the current processes.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.680
Well, you may say, wouldn't
that just need tools and steps?

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:29.730
Mostly, Yes.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.179
But we will also need to
understand the business reasons

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:35.659
so that we can accommodate
any changes that may end up

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:37.460
making the process better.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.220
We will also need to
understand the occurrence,

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.130
whether it happens
daily, weekly or ad hoc,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.280
so that we can choose the
best platform or tool for it.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.740
Lastly, we tend to
forget communication

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.470
as part of the automation,
and I would always

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.030
recommend saving a
few communication

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.430
examples in your document.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:59.030
On top of that, we also want
to know the current efforts

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:03.560
in terms of the overall time for
each run before the automation

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.920
and what's the manual versus
processing time breakdown

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.600
and identifying the tough
items through this process.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.730
Ideally, the automation
should always

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:18.500
work if we code things right.
But oftentimes it is not ideal,

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.090
which is why it
is critical for us

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:23.600
to know how often should we
update the process of document

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:27.260
so that we know when
it would be obsolete?

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:32.460
And oftentimes we are not always
the original report owner.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.210
So we need to know when to stop
and call for additional help.

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:40.320
Now all of these will get
us a detailed document

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:41.230
of the requirements.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.140
Of course, it's always
a great opportunity

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:47.070
to practice R markdown. But
other than the document itself,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.170
we now know

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.810
the complexity of
potentially automating

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:56.280
this process, the impact as
well as the stability, which

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.100
would allow you to rank all
your tasks in a table that

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:00.530
looks like this.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:03.080
So if you're wondering
which one to start,

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.780
you may not want to start
with task number one, where it

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.950
is complicated, has small impact,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.850
and may require you to update
the code every other week.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.360
You may want to consider
starting from automating task

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.120
number three, where it
is not overly complicated

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.810
will earn you some recognition
and there's less need for you

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:23.390
to update the code
and then maybe you

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.780
can move on to the harder ones.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.270
After knowing what is needed
and identifying the task

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:31.400
to start with, time to
roll up the sleeves.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.655
Now to do the actual automation,

00:10:33.655 --> 00:10:35.280
there are so many
things to cover here.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.990
In fact, I've been working
on an automation book trying

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.660
to cover all the
common scenarios using

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:41.930
two dozens of chapters.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:43.960
So I would just be brief
here with my top three

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.890
recommendations.

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:49.580
My first recommendation is to
always start with components.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.460
Let's just say if you have
a process that separately

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.370
involves SQL, Excel
and Outlook, you

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.310
want to code them one by one
because within the same team

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:01.340
and organization, the
same processes will

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.410
involve the similar
components where

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:05.960
you can just reuse the code.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.480
My second recommendation is
that we should definitely

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.160
do plenty of tests to capture
all the scenarios possible.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:17.120
Whether it be dependency
tests, user tests, dev tests,

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.380
unit tests.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.870
We should do all
the applicable ones.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.030
I know everybody
trusts their own code.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.080
I do too.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.580
But the reality has taught me
to trust the tests even more.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:32.950
Lastly, be practical
and stay on target.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.840
I know it feels great
to be able to build

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.990
all solutions within R.
But the thing is, not

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.090
everything needs to
be fully automated.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:41.480
At the end of the day,

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.680
it is not about building
something cool with R,

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.620
but building something
impactful with R.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.580
I've got lost in
that so many times.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.040
So let me say this again.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.500
It is not about building
something cool with R,

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.340
but building something
impactful with R.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.950
Now after the coding
task, it is all done

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:02.790
and we can now just hand
off process and move on.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:06.690
But there are still things we
need to look out for in order

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:08.670
to keep the project going.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:12.000
Overall, there are
three deadly situations

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.990
that could affect your project.

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:18.380
The first situation is
that after you handed off

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.410
your automated processes,
someone ran the code.

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.870
But the result isn't
what's expected.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:27.350
Because maybe because the
script wasn't handled properly,

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.040
like running on a new
machine or a new environment

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.290
without the proper setup.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.000
Believe it or not,
after all these years,

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.120
it still happens to me.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:40.420
So to avoid this situation,
what I would recommend

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:43.540
is to always have a handoff
document that discusses

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:45.640
the requirements of
the job, instructions on

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:48.100
running the process,
testing the process,

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.700
and what to do to maintain.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.980
The second scenario
is that as you

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.080
build more and more
automated processes,

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.710
chances are multiple
processes may run into issues

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:02.370
at the same time,
and sometimes someone

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.890
will come up and then
need your help to fix it.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:07.860
Sometimes multiple
people will show up,

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.930
which could look pressing.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.120
So my tip for you is this.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.550
Always discuss the failsafe
in your handoff document.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:19.590
In most cases, a line like
this will do the trick.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.330
This is almost like when the
autopilot stopped working.

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:23.890
You don't stop
flying or driving.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:28.680
You switch back to manual. And
this often reduces the urgency

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:32.670
and give us the time to
properly fix the issue.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.250
Lastly, everything went well.

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.690
You just got a new
feature request.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.550
Should you directly jump in?

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.350
And my recommendation here.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:43.700
Well, two recommendations.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:47.850
The short term is that always
treat it like a brand new task.

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.040
So that you can start from
gathering requirements.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.120
And then you can best
determine when to work on it.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.620
And then the longer
term solution

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:57.650
here is that it is also
a perfect opportunity

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:00.440
for you to train your individual
team members to gradually take

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.160
on the task and
accumulate more R knowledge.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.130
Now after you're comfortable
with dealing with those three

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:11.210
situations, we just need to
give it time for the project

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.010
to make progress.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:15.680
And just like any of the other
projects, from time to time,

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.660
you want to share updates.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:21.770
And to do that, I often
find it helpful to start

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:22.910
with the key stats.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.910
For example, hours saved, which
is something you can easily

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.560
extract from the
requirement document

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.830
that you have built in the
previous steps, and the number

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:33.020
of process documents
now available because

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:36.350
of this project, as well
as all the training that

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.135
happened because
throughout this project,

00:14:38.135 --> 00:14:40.760
whether it would be the official
one or the one-on-one sessions

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.080
that you had with
your team members.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.730
On top of that, you
also want to share

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.000
any of the success stories,
learnings that happen along

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.310
the way and maybe give
kudos to your team member

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.080
that you collaborated with.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:53.820
And lastly, you
don't want to forget

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:55.950
talking about the
hurdles where you

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.530
could use additional help on.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.770
Other than that, you also want
to determine a good cadence

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:03.090
to share progress.

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:06.060
For me, it was once a
month, but you should always

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:09.870
set up your own and know that
you can share progress whenever

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.420
you have critical updates.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:16.810
Now that wraps up the
key parts of the project.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.820
If you would like to
start something similar

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:23.030
at your workplace, this is a
structure you could follow.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:26.570
First, start from identifying
the task in your workplace,

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:30.080
then build your proposal
with the benefits

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:34.610
that would matter to your
decision makers and workplace.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.820
After that, build a requirement
document with possibly with R

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:42.040
Markdown, identify the
right task to start with.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:44.590
Then go by component.

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:50.330
Do plenty of tasks and stay on
target while at the same time,

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:54.240
try to stay away from the
three deadly situations.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:58.450
And don't forget to share
progress from time to time.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:01.450
Now I would like to ask
everyone to think back

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:03.720
about the original situation.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.870
As we build more and
more automated processes,

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:10.350
we have completely moved on
from the original question

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:14.820
of why the R adoption rate isn't
what's expected after the R

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.420
training to now having most
team members owning several

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:21.570
of these R processes where
they were at least involved

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:24.870
in building the requirements,
testing the process

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.690
and execution.

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:29.120
Rather than expecting
everyone to connect

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.910
the R functionalities
to the business needs,

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:35.870
through the R training, we
connect the business needs

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.780
to the R functionalities
through this project

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:42.900
and we just happen to
save thousands of hours.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.270
Now, if you're a decision
maker in this room,

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.550
this might be a good
project to make some impact

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.490
and accumulate some
R knowledge.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.400
If you're a team member in
this room, bring this back

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:55.890
and start from
identifying the tasks.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:59.010
If you're one of the two
team members in this room,

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:02.740
text your boss now and
say you have an idea.

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:04.470
Thank you.

